<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  Shell Citadel privacy manifest.

	  Apple requires this file even when the answer is "nothing", because silence is
	  not an answer to them. Ours is the easy case and it is worth saying why in the
	  file itself: this app has no account, no server of its own, no analytics and no
	  network destination other than the machine the user typed in themselves.

	  NSPrivacyTracking = false, and NSPrivacyTrackingDomains is deliberately EMPTY.
	  There is nothing to list. A photograph taken in this app goes over the user's own
	  SSH connection to the user's own Mac and is never written to the photo library.

	  ── LOCATION IS DECLARED BELOW, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE ────────────────────────
	  Added 2026-08-30 with the pin drop and the message stamp.

	  It is tempting to leave this empty on the reasoning that the data only ever goes
	  to the user's own computer. Apple's definition does not work that way: "collect"
	  means transmitted off the device, and the user's Mac is off the device. The
	  feature was described as an "undisclosed" stamp, and undisclosed can mean NOT
	  SHOWN IN THE MESSAGE BUBBLE without meaning NOT DECLARED TO APPLE. Those are
	  compatible, and only the first one is a design choice.

	  Undeclared location is one of the most reliably caught rejections there is.

	  Linked = false: there is no account, no identifier and no server, so there is
	  nothing for a coordinate to be linked TO. Tracking = false: it is never combined
	  with data from other companies, because it never reaches another company.

	  ⚠️ THE PHOTO-LIBRARY SENTENCE ABOVE IS STILL TRUE OF PHOTOGRAPHS and is now false
	  of one thing: a dropped pin's MAP PICTURE is saved to the library, because that
	  was asked for explicitly ("The snapshot goes in the thread and photo album").
	  Access is ADD-ONLY — the app cannot read the library. And the saved image carries
	  NO EXIF coordinates; see LocationStamp.swift.
	-->
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<!-- The silent stamp on every message: rounded to 2 dp (~1.1 km). -->
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeCoarseLocation</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<!-- The pin drop only, and only when the user taps it. -->
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePreciseLocation</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<!--
			  UserDefaults. Used for the connection profile and the byte offset the
			  reply channel resumes from. CA92.1 is the "app's own data, not shared
			  with anyone" reason, which is exactly what this is.
			-->
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>CA92.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
